PHISHING TRIAGE TOOLKIT
Project 3 – Phishing Awareness Analysis
Objective
Analyze common phishing attacks, identify warning signs, and provide a simple checklist and decision tree for non-technical users.
Sample Analysis
Sample 1: Mass Phishing
Scenario: Fake PayPal account suspension using a lookalike domain.
Red Flags: Generic greeting, fake domain, urgency, suspension threat.
Why Unsafe: Credential theft.
Verdict: Malicious
Sample 2: Spear Phishing
Scenario: Fake IT email requesting SSO renewal.
Red Flags: Similar-looking company domain, internal project references, external login page.
Why Unsafe: Steals company credentials.
Verdict: Malicious
Sample 3: Whaling/BEC
Scenario: Fake CEO requests confidential wire transfer.
Red Flags: Unknown domain, secrecy, deadline, avoids verification.
Why Unsafe: Financial fraud.
Verdict: Malicious
Sample 4: Smishing
Scenario: Fake package delivery SMS.
Red Flags: Unknown number, fake tracking site, urgency, no tracking ID.
Why Unsafe: Steals personal data.
Verdict: Malicious
Sample 5: Callback Phishing
Scenario: Fake Microsoft billing email asking to call support.
Red Flags: Fake sender, urgent payment, phone call instead of link.
Why Unsafe: Remote-access or payment scam.
Verdict: Malicious
Sample 6: Legitimate Email
Scenario: Internal project update.
Red Flags: Correct domain, no pressure, expected attachment.
Why Unsafe: Normal business communication.
Verdict: Safe
Non-Expert Triage Checklist
Check the sender's full email address.
Watch for lookalike domains and spelling changes.
Be cautious of urgency, fear, or secrecy.
Hover over links before clicking.
Never share passwords or MFA codes from email links.
Verify unusual requests through another trusted channel.
Report suspicious emails to the security team.
Decision Tree
1. Verify sender ? 2. Check content for pressure or unusual requests ? 3. Inspect links or attachments ? 4. If suspicious, report and do not interact; otherwise proceed normally.
Conclusion
Most phishing attacks rely on fake domains, urgency, or requests that bypass normal procedures. By checking the sender, verifying links, and reporting suspicious messages, users can avoid most phishing attempts.